//? Title: Bron–Kerbosch: Every Maximal Clique and the Excluded Set
//? Subtitle: Actual pivot frames, preserved discovery order and exponential output on multipartite graphs
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given an undirected graph, list every clique that cannot be extended by another
vertex. A maximal edge can coexist with a larger clique, and the number of
answers can grow exponentially. We retain the original seven-vertex example,
inspect actual candidate and excluded sets, and check a generated family with
exactly three choices per part. Native C++ supplies the cliques, pivot frames,
size histogram, work counts and multipartite inputs. The analysis distinguishes
maximality from maximum size and output volume from elapsed time.
)

\toc

> Original graph: a triangle, a four-clique and a maximal edge

The original graph contains the triangle {0,1,2}, the four-clique {2,3,4,5}
and the pendant edge {5,6}. All three are maximal: no outside vertex is
adjacent to every member. Only the four-clique is maximum, meaning largest
by cardinality. The pair {2,1} is a clique but is not maximal, since zero can
be added. That distinction will appear in a real excluded-set record below.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bron_kerbosch_graph.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> adjacency{{1,2},{0,2},{0,1,3,4,5},{2,4,5},{2,3,5},{2,3,4,6},{5}};
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bron_kerbosch_trace(7,adjacency);
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> position{{-4,2},{-4,-2},{0,0},{4,2},{4,-2},{8,0},{12,0}};
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  for (int u=0;u<7;++u) for (int v:adjacency[u]) if (u<v) {
    dm::graphics::DiagramStroke stroke;stroke.width=2.5;
    stroke.color=v<=2?dm::RGB{37,99,235}:u>=2 && v<=5?dm::RGB{22,163,74}:dm::RGB{217,119,6};
    diagram.path({position[u],position[v]},stroke);
  }
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.circle=true;style.width=.8;style.height=.8;style.padding=7;
  for (int v=0;v<7;++v) {
    style.fill=v<=1?dm::RGB{219,234,254}:v==2?dm::RGB{237,233,254}:v<=4?dm::RGB{220,252,231}:dm::RGB{255,237,213};
    diagram.node(std::format("v{}",v),position[v],std::format("{}",v),style);
  }
  diagram.text({-2,3.4},"triangle {0,1,2}",17,{37,99,235})
      .text({4,3.4},"4-clique {2,3,4,5}",17,{22,163,74})
      .text({10,-2},"maximal edge {5,6}",17,{217,119,6})
      .save_svg("build/bron_kerbosch_graph.svg");
  std::print("| discovery position | maximal clique | size |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<trace.cliques.size();++i)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",i,trace.cliques[i],trace.cliques[i].size());
  std::print("\nVertices: {}; unique undirected edges: {}; input adjacency entries: {}.\n",
      trace.vertex_count,trace.edge_count,trace.input_adjacency_entries);
}
```
