\caption(The original three maximal cliques and their discovery order are retained. Lines are undirected; an edge crossing without a labelled circle is not an additional vertex.)
\alttext(A blue triangle on vertices zero, one and two shares vertex two with a green four-clique on two, three, four and five. An orange edge from five to six cannot be extended to a larger clique.)

> Candidate and excluded sets: why both must be empty

A search frame maintains a clique R and two disjoint sets of vertices adjacent
to every vertex in R. P contains remaining candidates; X contains excluded
vertices already processed by preceding choices. X does not mean a list of
vertices that have already been reported as complete cliques.

Selecting v adds it to R and intersects both P and X with its neighbours.
After that child returns, the parent removes v from P and appends it to X.
A frame reports R exactly when both P and X are empty. If either contains a
vertex, that vertex can still extend R, so R is not maximal. Passing excluded
vertices into later children prevents rediscovering an earlier maximal extension.
This is the candidate/exclusion mechanism of
[Bron and Kerbosch's Algorithm 457](https://doi.org/10.1145/362342.362367).

Choose a pivot u from P union X that maximises its number of neighbours in P.
The actual branch snapshot is P minus N(u), in P order. To see why this covers
the remaining maximal extensions, consider an extension avoiding X. If it
contains u, it meets that snapshot because u is not adjacent to itself. If it
omits u and all its added vertices neighbour u, then u could extend it, contrary
to maximality. Thus a remaining maximal extension must meet the branch snapshot.
For u in X, an extension containing u is already excluded; the same argument
covers the others. This is a coverage argument, not a guarantee of small output.

The implementation scans P before X and retains the first maximum-scoring
pivot. The following records are actual recursion entries, captured before
each frame mutates its own P and X. R retains search insertion order; only
returned clique members are sorted. A later child's sets therefore reflect
preceding sibling choices, not merely its parent's unmodified snapshot.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bron_kerbosch.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> adjacency{{1,2},{0,2},{0,1,3,4,5},{2,4,5},{2,3,5},{2,3,4,6},{5}};
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bron_kerbosch_trace(7,adjacency);
  std::print("| frame | parent / selected v | R | P | X | pivot | branch snapshot | reports R |\n"
             "| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for (const auto& node:trace.nodes)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",node.id,
        node.id?std::format("{} / {}",node.parent,node.via_vertex):"root",node.r,node.p,node.x,
        node.pivot,node.branch_vertices,node.reported);
  std::print("\nActual calls: {}; branches: {}; maximum R size: {}.\n",
      trace.recursive_calls,trace.branches,trace.max_depth);
  std::print("Pivot-scoring probes: {}; branch-snapshot probes: {}; child-intersection probes: {}.\n\n",
      trace.pivot_tests,trace.branch_tests,trace.intersection_tests);
  std::vector<double> size,count;
  std::print("| clique size | maximal cliques |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t k=0;k<trace.clique_size_counts.size();++k) if (trace.clique_size_counts[k]) {
    size.push_back(k);count.push_back(trace.clique_size_counts[k]);
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n",k,trace.clique_size_counts[k]);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().bars(size,count,"maximal cliques",dm::RGB{5,150,105},.6)
      .title("Original graph: one maximal clique of each size 2, 3 and 4")
      .x_label("clique cardinality").y_label("number of maximal cliques")
      .y_limits(0,1.5).size(900,480).save_svg("build/bron_kerbosch.svg");
}
```
