\image(figures/bron_kerbosch_output_growth.svg)
// result_end
\caption(At 24 vertices, the search returns 6,561 cliques and enters 9,841 frames. Writing their member lists alone requires 8*6,561 vertex ids; a compact enumeration bound must not be confused with the cost of this explicit output.)
\alttext(The returned-clique and actual-call curves both rise exponentially across graphs with zero to twenty-four vertices. The call curve is about one and a half times the clique curve for the larger examples.)

If C is the actual number of calls and S counts returned vertex ids plus
clique headers, this vector/matrix implementation has conservative work
O(A+(n+1)²(C+1)+S log(n+2)), where A is the number of used input entries.
Pivot scoring scans up to n candidates against up to n vertices at a frame;
child intersections and vector copies add work, and emitted members are sorted.
The returned lists use O(S) storage. The adjacency matrix and value-copied
recursion sets use O(n²) storage, and retaining frame snapshots adds
O((n+1)C). On the constructed family, explicitly writing members already costs
Omega(q*3^q), before counting those search operations.

Output representation matters in general enumeration bounds. Tomita, Tanaka
and Takahashi's [2006 analysis](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2006.06.015)
uses a compact tree output format in its proof. Here the interface returns
full member lists, and the report accounts for that representation explicitly.

> Normalisation, boundary cases and a complete-call budget

The existing API interprets each used adjacency entry as an undirected edge,
even if only one orientation is supplied. It collapses duplicates, ignores
valid self-loops and uses only the first n rows; extra rows remain ignored.
The new validation checks that n is nonnegative, enough rows are supplied,
and every used endpoint is in [0,n), before allocating the matrix. Inputs are
borrowed for the call and never modified. Isolates are maximal singletons.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  struct Example {std::string name;int n;std::vector<std::vector<int>> adjacency;};
  const std::vector<Example> cases{
    {"zero vertices",0,{}},{"one isolate",1,{{}}},{"three isolates",3,{{},{},{}}},
    {"one-way input chain",3,{{1},{2},{}}},{"duplicates and loops",3,{{0,1,1,2},{2},{2}}},
    {"extra malformed row ignored",2,{{1},{},{-99}}}};
  std::print("| case | unique edges | maximal cliques |\n| :--- | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (const auto& example:cases) {
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bron_kerbosch_trace(example.n,example.adjacency);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",example.name,trace.edge_count,trace.cliques);
  }
  try {
    dm::algorithms::bron_kerbosch(3,{{1},{0}});return 1;
  } catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {
    std::print("\nToo few adjacency rows are rejected.\n");
  }
  try {
    dm::algorithms::bron_kerbosch(2,{{2},{}});return 1;
  } catch (const std::out_of_range&) {
    std::print("An out-of-range used endpoint is rejected.\n");
  }
  const auto adjacency=dm::algorithms::complete_multipartite_graph({3,3});
  try {
    dm::algorithms::bron_kerbosch_trace(6,adjacency,3);return 1;
  } catch (const std::runtime_error&) {
    std::print("A three-call budget rejects the incomplete search; no partial result is returned.\n");
  }
}
```
