| one-way input chain | 2 | [[0, 1], [1, 2]] |
| duplicates and loops | 3 | [[0, 1, 2]] |
| extra malformed row ignored | 1 | [[0, 1]] |

Too few adjacency rows are rejected.
An out-of-range used endpoint is rejected.
A three-call budget rejects the incomplete search; no partial result is returned.
// result_end
\caption(A one-way adjacency input is symmetrised, rather than treated as a directed-clique problem. The empty-graph clique and ignored-extra-row controls preserve the original interface's conventions.)

A positive trace call budget throws before entering an excess frame. It limits
search entries, not matrix allocation or total memory, and never returns a
truncated list as though enumeration were complete. Zero means unlimited.
Both ordinary and recorded APIs return owned lists; the ordinary API allocates
no diagnostic records. Large outputs, snapshots and recursion depth can still
be expensive, and allocation failures propagate.

> References and verification

C. Bron and J. Kerbosch (1973), *Algorithm 457: finding all cliques of an
undirected graph*, Communications of the ACM 16(9), 575–577,
[doi:10.1145/362342.362367](https://doi.org/10.1145/362342.362367).
The library uses deterministic maximum-neighbours-in-P pivot selection with
vector intersections and an adjacency matrix. E. Tomita, A. Tanaka and
H. Takahashi (2006), *The worst-case time complexity for generating all maximal
cliques and computational experiments*, Theoretical Computer Science 363,
28–42, [doi:10.1016/j.tcs.2006.06.015](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2006.06.015),
discusses enumeration complexity and output representation.

The worked graph is fully specified, and the generated family is deterministic;
no observational dataset or random seed is needed by the report. Native tests
check every undirected graph through six vertices against exhaustive subset
maximality, verify actual parent/sibling transitions and every logical counter,
and cover random asymmetric/duplicated inputs, boundary validation, budgets,
complete graphs and exact multipartite formulas. The existing SCC and clique
regressions also pass. Each block is an editable complete C++ programme; figures
are saved locally rather than printed as SVG text.
